## Verification rules and readiness

### Evidence before completion

A passing case must execute the specified behavior, check its observable outcome against an independent oracle and save the required artifacts. A mocked response proves only the orchestration exercised by that mock.

Treat every original action and expected result in the catalog as a minimum requirement. The setup, procedure, assertions, tier assignments and evidence fields make those requirements executable. Adjust proposed operational defaults through review before running the suite; do not quietly relax them after failure.

Protect acceptance tests, ground truth, scoring and policy fixtures from the candidate agent. Changes to these assets require a separate reviewed revision and invalidate results whose oracle has changed.

The overall readiness gate is 96 of 96 system cases plus 10 of 10 E2E cases, on the candidate revision, with the required real integrations and security boundaries exercised. A deterministic PR run alone does not establish live-provider or remote-worker readiness.

### Outcome semantics

| Result status | Meaning and gate effect |
| --- | --- |
| passed | Executed; every mandatory assertion passed and required evidence exists. |
| failed | Executed; an assertion, mandatory gate or evidence validation failed. |
| skipped | Intentionally not executed; requires a recorded reason and owner. Never a pass. |
| not_implemented | Audit confirms a required capability or executable test is absent. Never a pass. |
| blocked | Execution cannot proceed because a prerequisite is unavailable. Never a pass. |

Keep executionState separately as not_run or completed. Before execution, status may be null; once a result is published, status must be one of the five values above. Map a verifier's internal not_run state to executionState, never to passed. A missing runner or unimplemented test is recorded as not_implemented; unavailable credentials are blocked; an intentionally excluded profile is skipped.

### Failures and exceptions

Any required failed, skipped, blocked, not_implemented or not_run case prevents full acceptance. Temporary exceptions must identify owner, reason, risk, expiry and remediation; report the release as conditional rather than fully verified. Infrastructure errors cannot be converted into successful product behavior.

## Test levels and execution tiers

Test level describes what is checked. Execution tier describes where and when the test runs. They are separate fields in the acceptance registry.

| Level | Purpose | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Unit | Isolated logic | Router eligibility and budget arithmetic |
| Integration | Connected components | Tool result persisted by an agent task |
| End to end | Actual user workflow | Create a task, execute and review changes |
| Failure and resilience | Behavior under injected faults | Outage, crash, cancellation or reconnect |
| Security and performance | Boundaries and resource behavior | Unauthorized read; concurrent execution |

### Execution profiles

| Tier | Environment and dependencies | Schedule |
| --- | --- | --- |
| T0 | Pure logic, fixed clock and prices; no network | Every pull request |
| T1 | Real services and storage with deterministic providers | Every pull request |
| T2 | Packaged app or browser UI with fixture services | Relevant E2E on PR; full suite on main |
| T3 | Real disposable processes or containers; fault and security tests | Required boundary subset on PR; full nightly |
| T4 | Opt-in live providers and remote test workers; metered cost | Scheduled or manual; before readiness claim |

A case may have several profiles. Its result records the exact tier, components mocked, components real and environment. T4 supplements deterministic contract checks where actual model or remote compatibility must be demonstrated. Live credentials are injected only into approved test jobs.

### Proposed execution defaults

Use fixed seeds and clocks for deterministic scenarios. Repeat concurrency cases 20 times with recorded scheduling seeds; a single failure fails the case. Declare operation timeouts, cancellation deadlines, retry counts and performance budgets in the manifest before execution. Three retry attempts means three total attempts unless the manifest explicitly defines otherwise.

Set performance thresholds after measuring a versioned baseline on declared hardware; record p50 and p95 over at least 30 measured runs after warm-up. Reject a regression beyond the agreed budget. These samples are engineering checks, not a statistically conclusive ranking of model quality.

## Fixture inventory and ground truth

Run acceptance scenarios against small dedicated fixture repositories and disposable environments. Do not use the production ADE repository as the target for destructive, adversarial or coding-task fixtures.

| Fixture | Contents and independent oracle |
| --- | --- |
| F-CODE | Three Rust workspace crates, duplicate symbols, cross-module imports, shared utility and known callers. Keep the Cargo dependency graph acyclic; model intentional cycles in supported symbol or module references, or an explicitly invalid negative fixture. Pin expected edits. |
| F-PROVIDER | Two differently shaped provider adapters, scripted model responses, usage, prices, capabilities, tool calls and failure sequences. Include a compatible local endpoint. |
| F-MEMORY | Two project scopes, synthetic decisions, versions, source citations, paraphrases, contradictions and stale-source cases in resettable persistent storage. |
| F-WEB | Deterministic local site with forms, three routes, modal, delayed elements, controlled requests, HTTP 500 route and JavaScript error trigger. |
| F-PLUGIN | Valid sample plugin, disabled plugin, command collision, crashing plugin, custom adapter, incompatible API manifest and a local MCP server. |
| F-SECURITY | Disposable sandbox roots, allowed and forbidden sentinels, synthetic secrets, controlled network endpoints and scoped approval policy. |
| F-WORKER | Local process, container and remote worker harness with barriers, durable checkpoints, child process tracking, heartbeats and retry policy. |
| F-EVAL | Five protected coding tasks, versioned expected results, independent scoring, fixed configuration and deterministic response cassettes. |

### Ground truth contract

Keep manifest.json, symbols.json, references.json, dependency-graph.json, expected-diffs and expected-results under reviewed fixture versions. Each oracle records supported languages, symbol identities, file paths and spans, relevant tests and expected outcomes. Hash all protected inputs before and after a run.

The evaluator and candidate must use separate permission scopes. Candidate workspaces are writable only where edits are intended; the evaluator reads protected tests from an independent location. Do not rely on an agent promise to leave its own tests unchanged.

## Fixture lifecycle and deterministic controls

### Create and reset

Allocate a unique run ID, case ID, attempt ID, workspace, datastore namespace and ports. Materialize a fixture from its pinned immutable version. Preserve the base commit and any intentional user-change sentinel.

Verify readiness through service health signals and barriers. Reset storage, browser context, tool outputs and queues before each case. Do not use arbitrary sleeps to hide missing readiness checks.

After the case, capture results and evidence before cleanup. Terminate tracked processes and release containers, ports, temporary credentials and remote allocations. A cleanup failure is reported explicitly and fails the required cleanup assertion.

### Provider simulator behavior

| Control | Required scenarios |
| --- | --- |
| Responses | Valid text and tool calls; malformed payloads; unsupported capabilities |
| Transport | HTTP 429, 500 and 503; timeout; interrupted stream; artificial latency |
| Limits | Retry-After, rate limits, context-window rejection and budget exhaustion |
| Accounting | Variable input, output and cached token usage; delayed actual usage; versioned prices |
| Replay | Pinned request expectations, recorded responses, virtual clock and deterministic seed |

### Five coding evaluation fixtures

| Task | Protected correctness check |
| --- | --- |
| Bug repair | Fix a boundary case without breaking normal inputs. |
| Small feature | Add a declared behavior and validate success and error cases. |
| Cross-file refactor | Rename a symbol and preserve callers across packages. |
| UI interaction | Implement and verify a deterministic form workflow. |
| Failure recovery | Handle a provider or worker fault under a bounded policy. |

These are smoke tasks. Report per-task correctness, latency and cost separately and show the dataset version and sample size. Expanding to a model-quality benchmark requires more tasks, multiple runs, control of confounders and an explicit uncertainty analysis.

## Test infrastructure and repository layout

APEX is developed mainly in Rust on the backend. Use Cargo workspace tests and the existing async runtime for backend verification. Keep unit tests beside their Rust modules and integration tests in a dedicated workspace test crate. Retain Playwright for an existing web frontend; it does not replace Rust backend tests. Use disposable processes or containers for isolation and the existing CI service.

```text
Cargo.toml                    # workspace manifest
Cargo.lock
rust-toolchain.toml            # pinned supported toolchain
crates/
  <backend-crate>/src/
    model_routing.rs           # unit tests in #[cfg(test)]
    budget_policy.rs
    permissions.rs
  acceptance-tests/
    Cargo.toml                # registered workspace member
    src/lib.rs                # shared harness utilities
    tests/
      model_providers.rs
      agent_execution.rs
      context_memory.rs
      plugin_system.rs
      security.rs
      resilience.rs
xtask/
  Cargo.toml                  # proposed runner package
  src/main.rs
tests/
  acceptance/systems.yaml
  fixtures/                   # code, web, providers, workers
  benchmarks/
    fixtures/
    expected-results/
  reports/
ui-tests/                     # only if a web UI exists
  coding-workflow.spec.ts
  browser-verification.spec.ts
```

Cargo discovers integration targets in a package's tests directory; nested suites need an explicit module or test target. Register both test and runner crates as workspace members. Add deterministic clocks, provider simulators, process cleanup, evidence validation and fixture hashes to the shared harness. Reference: https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/commands/cargo-test.html

## Acceptance runner and execution contract

### Registry and discovery

systems.yaml is the authoritative inventory of all 106 case IDs. It maps each ID to its system, specification, executable test, level, required profiles, fixture versions, owner, prerequisites, deadlines, assertion names and artifacts. Discovery must reject missing IDs, duplicate IDs and a test file with no meaningful assertions.

```yaml
id: MA-04
system: multi-agent-orchestration
required: true
level: integration
profiles: [deterministic]
fixtures: [F-CODE]
testFile: crates/acceptance-tests/tests/agent_execution.rs
testName: ma_04_conflicting_edits
assertions: [conflictDetected, noSilentOverwrite]
evidence: [log.json, diff-A.patch, diff-B.patch]
repetitions: 20
timeoutMs: 60000
```

### Proposed command interface

Implement an xtask workspace binary with the application arguments below, or document an equivalent Rust runner. These are proposed interfaces, not existing Cargo subcommands or implemented scripts. Substitute each catalog ID for MA-04 in the first command. The runner maps IDs to Rust test names and validates discovery and evidence.

```bash
cargo run --locked -p xtask -- acceptance --id MA-04
cargo run --locked -p xtask -- acceptance --system MA
cargo run --locked -p xtask -- acceptance --all
cargo run --locked -p xtask -- acceptance --all --profile security
cargo run --locked -p xtask -- acceptance --all --profile live
cargo run --locked -p xtask -- report --run-id <runId>
```

Default the runner to the deterministic profile. Require an explicit --allow-billable flag in addition to --profile live before any metered provider call; remote-only live runs need no provider billing permission. Preserve command exit status and reject a filter that executes zero expected tests.

Profiles map to tiers: deterministic includes T0, T1 and local T2; security executes T3; live executes T4. If a case needs more than one profile, retain separate results and aggregate only after every required profile passes. Repetitions remain one catalog case with all attempts retained.

### Runner invariants

- Return a nonzero exit when any required selected case fails, cannot run or lacks evidence. Never turn a missing executable into an empty passing suite.

- Write a result for every planned case, even after runner interruption. Reconcile abandoned runs as blocked or failed with executionState not_run or completed as appropriate.

- Use runtime events and independent filesystem, process or network observations as assertions. Do not accept an agent's narrative as the sole oracle.

- Protect the report writer from candidate edits; validate schemas, artifact existence and hashes before assigning passed.

## CI gates and release acceptance

| Trigger | Required work | Gate effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Every pull request | Formatting and static analysis; T0 and T1; relevant T2; required T3 boundary cases; coverage and evidence validation | Any mandatory failure or silent omission blocks green completion. |
| Main branch and nightly | Full deterministic suite; all local E2E; repeated concurrency, resilience and security profiles | Publish defects and prevent readiness claims for the affected build. |
| Scheduled or manual | T4 live-provider smoke, full remote environments and expensive performance suites | Meter cost and require credentials; blocked profiles remain visible. |
| Release candidate | All 106 IDs and all required profiles on the exact candidate revision; full evidence and defect review | Require 8/8 per system and 10/10 E2E for full acceptance. |

### Mandatory pull request checks

Formatting and static analysis pass; unit tests pass; integration tests pass; relevant E2E tests pass; required security tests pass; no required test is silently skipped; coverage is produced; results and artifacts are saved; failed mandatory checks prevent a green completion status.

### Rust backend checks

```bash
cargo fmt --all -- --check
cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings
cargo test --workspace --locked
cargo llvm-cov --workspace --locked --lcov --output-path lcov.info
```

Pin compatible tooling and include documentation tests. Declare supported feature and target combinations; do not assume --all-features is valid when features conflict. Use the project's async test runtime and exercise cancellation and task cleanup. Sources: https://doc.rust-lang.org/clippy/usage.html and https://github.com/taiki-e/cargo-llvm-cov

### Selection and protection

Declare required test selection before execution using change impact plus an always-required safety core. Run the full deterministic suite on main to detect omissions in selective PR coverage. Require separate review for changes to acceptance criteria, ground truth, CI gates and security policy. A passing result for an earlier commit does not verify a changed candidate.

### Reruns and flaky behavior

Retain the original failure, classify its cause and rerun only after preserving evidence. A passing retry does not erase a nondeterministic failure. Record quarantine with an owner and expiry, and keep required quarantined cases out of the passed count. After a legitimate fix, rerun the case and affected regression suites.

### Budgets and performance

Use atomic shared reservations before billable dispatch. Stop new calls when estimated allowance is exhausted, then reconcile lagged actual usage. In-flight calls can overrun estimates. Measure performance on declared hardware and pinned workload; enforce configured regression budgets without mistaking mocked latency for live-provider performance.

## Evidence records and artifact formats

Every execution produces a structured record, human-readable report and linked artifacts. The following record illustrates the supplied format; its values are examples, not measured APEX results.

```json
{
  "testId": "MA-04",
  "system": "multi-agent-orchestration",
  "status": "passed",
  "executionType": "integration",
  "environment": "isolated-worktrees",
  "mockedComponents": [],
  "durationMs": 1840,
  "assertions": {
    "conflictDetected": true,
    "silentOverwrite": false
  },
  "artifacts": [
    "reports/MA-04/log.json",
    "reports/MA-04/diff.patch"
  ]
}
```

### Required provenance extensions

Add schemaVersion, runId, attemptId, executionState, commitSha, dirtyTreeHash if applicable, startedAt and finishedAt in UTC, tier, profile, realComponents, fixtureVersions and hashes, runtime and container versions, provider and model IDs, configuration hash, seed, declared timeouts, cost estimate, actual usage availability and exit code. Every nonpassing result includes reason, defect ID or prerequisite and next action.

### Artifact conventions

Store tests/reports/<runId>/<testId>/<attemptId>/result.json plus events.jsonl, stdout.log, stderr.log, diff.patch, trace.zip, screenshots and metrics.json as applicable. A manifest lists relative paths, media types, byte sizes and SHA-256 hashes. JUnit XML supports CI summaries; JSON is the authoritative result format; HTML or Markdown supplies the review view.

Redact synthetic and real secret values before persistence. Validate artifact links and checksums before reporting. Protect evaluation artifacts from candidate writes. Set retention and access rules in CI configuration; proposed defaults are 30 days for routine successful runs and 90 days for failed and release runs, subject to project policy.

## Reporting and verification coverage

### System dashboard

For each system show implemented capabilities, passed, failed, missing, blocked and skipped cases, latest execution time, tested revision, real versus mocked scope, evidence links, known defects and remaining work. Never calculate completeness using only tests that happened to run.

| Metric | Definition |
| --- | --- |
| System acceptance | passed required cases / 8; all required profiles must pass for a case to count. |
| Workflow acceptance | passed required E2E cases / 10. |
| Overall acceptance | passed required cases / 106, retaining missing and blocked cases in the denominator. |
| Execution coverage | cases actually executed / 106; distinct from passing and source-code coverage. |
| Implementation coverage | capabilities and tests mapped to implementation files; unknown until audited. |
| Code coverage | Rust line, region and function coverage from cargo-llvm-cov; branch coverage only if supported by the pinned setup. Record thresholds, exclusions and separate frontend coverage. |

### Required report outputs

| Deliverable | Minimum content |
| --- | --- |
| TESTING_ARCHITECTURE.md | Stack, harness boundaries, fixtures, profiles, isolation and commands. |
| ACCEPTANCE_MATRIX.md | All 106 IDs, mapped files, test functions, profiles, prerequisites and status. |
| TEST_RESULTS.md | Run manifest, counts, outcomes, evidence links and readiness verdict. |
| KNOWN_DEFECTS.md | Reproduction, severity, owner, cause, impact, remediation and rerun. |
| TEST_COVERAGE.md | Requirement coverage, code coverage, unsupported paths and exclusions. |
| Executable assets | Automated suite, CI configuration, fixtures, utilities and machine-readable results. |

### Verification language

Use four separate capability labels: implemented, mock-only verified, real integration verified and unverified. Pair each verified label with run ID, case IDs, revision, environment and evidence. A source-code coverage percentage or an attractive dashboard does not substitute for acceptance results.

## Implementation phases and responsibilities

| Phase | Work and exit condition |
| --- | --- |
| 1 Repository audit | Identify architecture and tests; map 12 systems; record missing capabilities and create the baseline coverage matrix before new product features. |
| 2 Testing platform | Build fixture lifecycle, deterministic provider, worker harness, evidence storage, coverage and CI using the actual stack. Demonstrate a positive and negative control. |
| 3 Acceptance cases | Implement every catalog ID with observable assertions and evidence. Missing capabilities stay not_implemented. |
| 4 Deterministic testing | Exercise response and fault scenarios with fixed fixtures; separately label opt-in live API smoke tests and cost. |
| 5 Security validation | Exercise actual process or container boundaries and adversarial routes in disposable environments. |
| 6 CI and gates | Wire mandatory checks and report validation; prove a deliberate failure prevents green completion. |
| 7 Dashboard | Generate system counts, missing work, defects, execution time and evidence without hiding nonpassing cases. |
| 8 Execute and report | Run suites; classify failures; preserve evidence; fix scoped defects; rerun cases and regressions; publish remaining gaps. |

### Accountability

The coding agent implements tests and scoped fixes. The acceptance owner approves requirements and justified changes. The reviewer checks oracle integrity and evidence. CI executes the protected gates and stores results. The candidate agent must not independently weaken its own acceptance criteria.

### Failure triage

Classify every failure as product implementation, test implementation, specification ambiguity or environment. Preserve the failing input, run metadata, logs and patch. A changed test needs an explicit reason and review; a product fix needs a rerun on the corrected revision. Finish with a verified-status table for all 12 systems and the E2E suite.

### Catalog reading guide

Each catalog case retains the original action and expected result. Setup and procedure specify reproducible execution; additional assertions make failure measurable. The evidence field lists case-specific artifacts in addition to the shared result record. Use the runner command with the displayed ID. Multi-tier cases require their declared profiles before full acceptance.

## System 1 Model-agnostic AI engine

Goal: Connect multiple model providers, switch models, and route tasks intelligently.

### AI-01 Adapter normalization

**Profile and fixtures:** T1  |  F-PROVIDER

**Action:** Connect two mock providers using different API adapters

**Expected:** Both return normalized responses

**Setup:** Configure two adapters with different request and response schemas and equivalent scripted outputs.

**Procedure:** Invoke both through the public task service; compare content, tool calls, usage fields and finish reasons against a canonical response.

**Pass assertions:** Requests reach the correct adapter once; normalized fields match the contract and no provider-specific fields are required downstream.

**Evidence:** Request envelopes and normalized responses.

### AI-02 Common task interface

**Profile and fixtures:** T1  |  F-CODE F-PROVIDER

**Action:** Run the same coding task with two different models

**Expected:** Both execute through the same task interface

**Setup:** Reset two isolated copies of the same coding fixture; bind each to a different scripted model.

**Procedure:** Submit identical tasks through the same interface and run the fixture acceptance command in each copy.

**Pass assertions:** Both accepted changes satisfy the fixture oracle; task lifecycle and output schema match despite different provider payloads.

**Evidence:** Task requests diffs and test output.

### AI-03 Model switching with state

**Profile and fixtures:** T1  |  F-PROVIDER

**Action:** Change the selected model during a session

**Expected:** Next model call uses the new selection without losing stored conversation state

**Setup:** Start a session with model A and persist two conversation turns.

**Procedure:** Switch to B, submit a follow-up, and inspect its request and stored transcript.

**Pass assertions:** Only the next call uses B; prior turns remain ordered, complete and attributed to their original model.

**Evidence:** Selection events request routing and transcript.

### AI-04 Rate limit handling

**Profile and fixtures:** T1  |  F-PROVIDER

**Action:** Simulate provider rate limit (HTTP 429)

**Expected:** Backoff/retry policy runs and respects retry limits

**Setup:** Script 429 responses with Retry-After and configure a three-attempt ceiling and virtual clock.

**Procedure:** Run one recoverable sequence and one sequence that exhausts retries; advance time deterministically.

**Pass assertions:** Attempt times obey policy; total attempts never exceed three; exhaustion produces a terminal failure with no retry loop.

**Evidence:** Attempt timeline headers and terminal state.

Cases AI-01 through AI-08 form one eight-case completion gate.

Completion gate: 8/8 pass, with recorded provider requests, normalized outputs, model selections, and cost estimates. Budget enforcement must acknowledge that provider billing reports may lag and in-flight calls can exceed estimates.

### AI-05 Provider outage handling

**Profile and fixtures:** T1  |  F-PROVIDER

**Action:** Simulate provider outage (HTTP 503)

**Expected:** Task fails clearly or uses an explicitly configured fallback

**Setup:** Script 503 responses; prepare separate configurations with fallback disabled and explicitly enabled.

**Procedure:** Execute both configurations and inspect routed calls and final task states.

**Pass assertions:** Disabled fallback fails clearly; enabled fallback uses only the permitted provider and records the switch and any retry limit.

**Evidence:** Provider trace fallback event and error record.

### AI-06 Capability validation

**Profile and fixtures:** T0 T1  |  F-PROVIDER

**Action:** Require tool calling from a model that lacks the capability

**Expected:** System rejects the configuration or uses a documented alternative

**Setup:** Declare a model without tool-calling capability and a task that requires a tool.

**Procedure:** Submit the task through configuration validation and the execution entry point.

**Pass assertions:** Unsupported execution is rejected before dispatch, or the documented alternative is used and observable; no fabricated tool result appears.

**Evidence:** Capability matrix validation error and dispatch count.

### AI-07 Budget exhaustion

**Profile and fixtures:** T0 T1  |  F-PROVIDER

**Action:** Set a maximum API budget of $0.50

**Expected:** No new billable calls are dispatched after the estimated remaining allowance is exhausted

**Setup:** Set allowance to USD 0.50; use fixed prices and estimates with known reservations and an in-flight request.

**Procedure:** Dispatch calls until remaining allowance cannot cover the next estimate, then attempt another call.

**Pass assertions:** The new billable call is rejected; ledger totals and reservations explain the decision; lagged actual charges remain visible.

**Evidence:** Reservation ledger denied dispatch and estimate reconciliation.

### AI-08 Local endpoint support

**Profile and fixtures:** T1 T2  |  F-PROVIDER

**Action:** Test a local model endpoint

**Expected:** Compatible endpoint works without requiring a hosted-provider account

**Setup:** Start a compatible local model endpoint and remove all hosted-provider credentials from the test environment.

**Procedure:** Run a task through the local adapter and inspect endpoint logs and outbound network records.

**Pass assertions:** The local endpoint returns a valid normalized result; no hosted account or unintended hosted request is required.

**Evidence:** Local endpoint transcript and network log.

## System 2 Multi-agent orchestration

Goal: Run independent agents concurrently, coordinate dependencies, and safely integrate changes.

### MA-01 Concurrent workers

**Profile and fixtures:** T1  |  F-CODE F-WORKER

**Action:** Launch three independent tasks

**Expected:** All three can run concurrently

**Setup:** Create three independent jobs with controllable start barriers and separate workspaces.

**Procedure:** Release all jobs together and hold each at a checkpoint until the others have started.

**Pass assertions:** Worker activity intervals overlap for all three; each job has its own result and reaches its expected terminal state.

**Evidence:** Worker timeline workspace ids and outputs.

### MA-02 Dependency ordering

**Profile and fixtures:** T0 T1  |  F-WORKER

**Action:** Create A  >  B  >  C dependencies

**Expected:** B cannot start before A succeeds; C waits for B

**Setup:** Define the DAG A to B to C with controlled completion and failure responses.

**Procedure:** Complete A then B; repeat with A failing and with cancellation while B waits.

**Pass assertions:** B starts only after A succeeds, C only after B succeeds; failure or cancellation blocks descendants without running them.

**Evidence:** Dag events start timestamps and blocked states.

### MA-03 Independent edits

**Profile and fixtures:** T1  |  F-CODE

**Action:** Two agents edit different files

**Expected:** Both sets of changes remain intact

**Setup:** Fork two workers from the same fixture revision and assign distinct files with known expected patches.

**Procedure:** Run concurrently, integrate both results and compare the resulting tree to the combined expected patches.

**Pass assertions:** Both edits survive exactly; unrelated files and user-owned sentinel changes remain unchanged.

**Evidence:** Base revision both patches and integrated tree hash.

### MA-04 Conflicting edits

**Profile and fixtures:** T1  |  F-CODE

**Action:** Two agents edit the same function

**Expected:** Conflict detected and escalated; neither change silently overwritten

**Setup:** Assign incompatible changes to the same function in two isolated workers.

**Procedure:** Release both workers concurrently and attempt integration without providing conflict resolution.

**Pass assertions:** Conflict is reported with both candidate patches; integration does not silently select or overwrite either change.

**Evidence:** Conflict report both patches and branch state.

Cases MA-01 through MA-08 form one eight-case completion gate.

Completion gate: 8/8 pass. Run concurrency tests repeatedly, not just once, to catch race conditions.

### MA-05 Scoped delegation

**Profile and fixtures:** T1  |  F-WORKER F-SECURITY

**Action:** Agent A delegates a subtask to B

**Expected:** B receives bounded context, instructions, and permission scope

**Setup:** Give parent A public context plus a private sentinel and a narrower child permission policy.

**Procedure:** Delegate to B and inspect its actual input envelope; attempt an out-of-scope child operation.

**Pass assertions:** B receives required instructions and bounded context; private sentinel is absent and the narrower scope is enforced.

**Evidence:** Delegation envelope permission decisions and child trace.

### MA-06 Worker crash isolation

**Profile and fixtures:** T1 T3  |  F-WORKER

**Action:** Terminate one worker during execution

**Expected:** Other independent workers continue; failed task gets a clear state

**Setup:** Run three independent workers with separate process identifiers and a checkpoint in each.

**Procedure:** Kill one worker after it starts; allow the other two to finish.

**Pass assertions:** Only the killed task becomes interrupted or failed according to policy; surviving jobs retain their outputs and complete.

**Evidence:** Process inventory worker exit and surviving results.

### MA-07 Parent cancellation

**Profile and fixtures:** T1 T3  |  F-WORKER

**Action:** Cancel a parent task

**Expected:** Applicable child tasks and processes are cancelled or safely drained

**Setup:** Start a parent with active and queued children plus a long-running child subprocess.

**Procedure:** Cancel the parent through the normal API and wait for the declared cancellation deadline.

**Pass assertions:** Queued children never start; active work stops or drains under the documented policy; no orphan or false completion remains.

